# __PROJECT__

C++__CPP_STANDARD__ project using G++, CMake 3.28+ and Ninja, with a
`libs/core` library target, a thin `app` executable, and Catch2 tests.

```bash
cmake --preset dev
cmake --build --preset dev
./build/dev/bin/__PROJECT__
ctest --preset dev
```

| Preset | Purpose | Directory |
|---|---|---|
| `dev` | Debug with AddressSanitizer and UBSan | `build/dev` |
| `release` | Optimized | `build/release` |
| `gdb` | Debug without sanitizers, for clean stepping in GDB | `build/gdb` |

Warnings and sanitizers are applied through the `__NAMESPACE___warnings` and
`__NAMESPACE___sanitize` interface targets, so they affect only this project's targets.
Pass `-D__NAMESPACE_UPPER___WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS=ON` to promote warnings to errors. MinGW
does not ship the sanitizer runtimes; use the `gdb` preset there.

clangd reads `build/dev/compile_commands.json`, so configure `dev` once before editing.
`.vscode/launch.json` debugs the `gdb` build. `cmake --install build/dev` copies the
executable into `~/.local/bin` (or `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Microsoft\WindowsApps` on Windows)
unless you pass `--prefix`.

The sample `core::greeting()` function and its Catch2 case exist only to prove the
scaffold builds and tests end to end — replace them with real logic.
